codex.problem:
The repeated loop transfer treats a function-local qregion declaration as a fresh dynamic declaration each iteration. Reproduced hash rejection '§3.3: region name already defined' for the memoryless action with signature mut=[1],in=[],args=[],mem=[],bounds=[2]:
action @a (t:wire mut) () () () ()
regs {  } bounds {2}
^entry():
qloop 2 {
qregion r 1 1
}
There is one lexical declaration of a two-cell zero region, no mutations, and no leaked workspace. §4.6 creates this region once at function entry. action.rs already gathers/allocates it once at entry and treats its body occurrence as a no-op; validation instead inserts the same name twice. The earlier one-pass loop validator accepted this case, so fixing iteration analysis introduced a rejection regression.

Malformed predicate nesting reaches expect("qif predicate") at validation.rs:111. Reproduced hash exit 101 and a Rust panic for a memoryless action with mut=[1],in=[1],args=[],bounds=[]:
action @a (t:wire mut) (p:wire in) () () ()
^entry():
qif p {
qunpred p
} {
x t
}
The first arm consumes the qif-owned predicate. Before validating the second arm, last_mut().expect(...) assumes that predicate remains. This is a malformed-object rejection path, not an internal invariant. The round-1 implementation checked its final pop and returned the §3.5 error for this case; the new polarity update introduced the panic.
